For handling FTP (File Transfer Protocol) and SFTP (Secure File Transfer Protocol) in Java,
there are several libraries available:



FTP Libraries:
    These libraries are used for standard FTP operations (upload, download, delete, rename, etc.).

Apache Commons Net (Popular & Lightweight)

Library: commons-net

Features:
        FTP and FTPS (FTP over SSL)
        Simple to use for basic FTP tasks

Dependency (Maven)

<dependency>
    <groupId>commons-net</groupId>
    <artifactId>commons-net</artifactId>
    <version>3.9.0</version>
</dependency>



EXAMPLE:

import org.apache.commons.net.ftp.FTP;
import org.apache.commons.net.ftp.FTPClient;
import java.io.FileInputStream;
import java.io.IOException;

public class FTPUploadExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String server = "ftp.example.com";
        int port = 21;
        String user = "username";
        String pass = "password";

        FTPClient ftpClient = new FTPClient();
        try {
            ftpClient.connect(server, port);
            ftpClient.login(user, pass);
            ftpClient.enterLocalPassiveMode();
            ftpClient.setFileType(FTP.BINARY_FILE_TYPE);

            String remoteFile = "/uploads/test.txt";
            FileInputStream inputStream = new FileInputStream("test.txt");

            boolean done = ftpClient.storeFile(remoteFile, inputStream);
            inputStream.close();

            if (done) {
                System.out.println("File uploaded successfully.");
            }
        } catch (IOException ex) {
            ex.printStackTrace();
        } finally {
            try {
                ftpClient.logout();
                ftpClient.disconnect();
            } catch (IOException ex) {
                ex.printStackTrace();
            }
        }
    }
}




SFTP Libraries:


SFTP uses SSH (Secure Shell) protocol, and standard Java does not support it natively.

JSch (Java Secure Channel)

Library: jsch

Features:
        Secure File Transfer Protocol (SFTP)
        Secure Shell / SSH key Authentication

 Dependency (Maven):

 <dependency>
     <groupId>com.jcraft</groupId>
     <artifactId>jsch</artifactId>
     <version>0.1.55</version>
 </dependency>




EXAMPLE:
import com.jcraft.jsch.*;

import java.io.FileInputStream;

public class SFTPUploadExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String host = "sftp.example.com";
        int port = 22;
        String user = "username";
        String password = "password";
        String localFile = "test.txt";
        String remoteFile = "/uploads/test.txt";

        try {
            JSch jsch = new JSch();
            Session session = jsch.getSession(user, host, port);
            session.setPassword(password);
            session.setConfig("StrictHostKeyChecking", "no");
            session.connect();

            Channel channel = session.openChannel("sftp");
            channel.connect();
            ChannelSftp sftpChannel = (ChannelSftp) channel;

            FileInputStream inputStream = new FileInputStream(localFile);
            sftpChannel.put(inputStream, remoteFile);
            inputStream.close();

            sftpChannel.exit();
            session.disconnect();

            System.out.println("File uploaded successfully.");
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}






Apache Mina SSHD

Library: mina-sshd

Features:

        SFTP and SSH server implementation
        Supports both client and server functionalities

Dependency (Maven)


<dependency>
    <groupId>org.apache.sshd</groupId>
    <artifactId>sshd-sftp</artifactId>
    <version>2.10.0</version>
</dependency>





SSHJ


Library: sshj
Features:
        Secure SSH connections
        Supports SCP & SFTP


Dependency (Maven):

    <dependency>
        <groupId>com.hierynomus</groupId>
        <artifactId>sshj</artifactId>
        <version>0.34.0</version>
    </dependency>




Which One Should You Use?
For FTP → Use Apache Commons Net (commons-net)
For SFTP → Use JSch (lightweight) or SSHJ (modern alternative)
For advanced needs (e.g., SSH server, custom handling) → Use Apache Mina SSHD





